title: 'rpc_server: allow setting `TCP_NODELAY` on JSON-RPC connections'
doc:
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    ### Summary
    The JSON-RPC server never sets `TCP_NODELAY`, so [Nagle's algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagle%27s_algorithm) stays enabled on every RPC socket. jsonrpsee sets the option in its own accept loop, but that loop never runs: substrate accepts connections itself and hands jsonrpsee an already-accepted socket.

    CI runtime for `revive-differential-tests` rose to ~2 hours after the subxt 0.50 bump moved eth-rpc onto `chainHead`; the main cause is the latency between the messages a `chainHead` call sends.

    ### Change
    1. Add a `tcp_nodelay` field to `RpcEndpoint`, applied to each connection as it is accepted.
    2. Add `--rpc-tcp-nodelay`, and a `tcp-nodelay=` key for `--experimental-rpc-endpoint`. The two conflict, as the other per-endpoint settings already do.
    3. Default to off, leaving existing nodes unchanged.
    4. Enable it for `revive-dev-node`.
crates:
- name: sc-cli
  bump: major
- name: sc-rpc-server
  bump: major
- name: sc-service
  bump: major
- name: cumulus-client-cli
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: major
